Q: unix timestamp of a given calendar week A: <p>If your goal is just to get that week-start timestamp, you don't actually have to calculate the correct ISO week number and convert it back, you can just do something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>sub week_start {
    my ($ts) = @_;
    my $ONE_DAY = 86400;
    my $week_day = (localtime $ts)[6];
    $week_day = ($week_day + 6) % 7; # Convert from 0=Sunday to 0=Monday
    return $ts - $week_day * $ONE_DAY - ($ts % $ONE_DAY);
}
</code></pre>
<p>which works by figuring out how many seconds there are between <code>$ts</code> and midnight of the most recent Monday, and subtracting that value from <code>$ts</code>, giving the timestamp of the week start.</p>
<p>On the other hand, if you do need the correct week-year and week-number for other purposes (perhaps display) then I would recommend using <a href="https://metacpan.org/pod/DateTime" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DateTime</a>, which has <code>$dt-&gt;week</code> to compute them directly (including the correct year), a CLDR formatter that can produce <code>YYYY-ww</code>, and <code>$dt-&gt;truncate(to =&gt; 'week')-&gt;epoch</code> to get the week-start timestamp.</p>

Q: Dafny restriction to ordered types A: <p>The standard library adopts a predicate based solution requiring you pass in a predicate function which provides a total ordering. However, there is no super type for numbers unfortunately. My guess for the reason is that each number type is mapped to a different underlying z3/smt theory and so it can't reason about numbers correctly concretely if we abstract over all number types. You can define abstract modules that are generic over an encapsulated type with implementations and then create concrete instances for each number type as needed.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>
abstract module Medians {
    import opened Std.Relations
    import opened Std.Collections.Seq
    type T(==,!new)

    predicate Lte(a: T, b: T)

    function add(a: T, b: T): T
    function halve(a: T): T

    function Median(xs: seq&lt;T&gt;): T 
        requires TotalOrdering(Lte)
        requires |xs| &gt; 0
    {
        var ss := MergeSortBy(Lte, xs);
        if |ss| % 2 == 0 then halve(add(ss[|ss|/2-1], ss[|ss|/2])) else ss[|ss|/2]
    }
}

module IntMedian refines Medians {
    type T = int

    function add(a: T, b: T):T {
        a+b
    }

    function halve(a: T):T {
        a / 2
    }

    predicate Lte(a: T, b:T ) {
        a &lt;= b
    }
}

module BvMedian refines Medians {
    type T = bv8

    function add(a: T, b: T):T {
        a+b
    }

    function halve(a: T):T {
        a &gt;&gt; 1
    }

    predicate Lte(a: T, b:T ) {
        a &lt;= b
    }
}

module TestMedians {
    import IM = IntMedian
    import BVM = BvMedian

    method Main() {
        var ints := [1,2,3,4,5];
        print &quot;\nints := &quot;, IM.Median(ints);
        var bvs: seq&lt;bv8&gt; := [1,2,3,4,5];
        print &quot;\nbit vectors := &quot;, BVM.Median(bvs);
    }
}

    predicate lteRange(left: (nat, nat), right: (nat, nat)) {
        left.1 &lt; right.1 || (left.1 == right.1 &amp;&amp; left.0 &lt;= right.0)
    }

    lemma IsTotalOrder()
        ensures TotalOrdering(lteRange)
    {}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://github.com/dafny-lang/dafny/blob/master/Source/DafnyStandardLibraries/src/Std/Relations.dfy#L56" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Total Ordering Definition</a></p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/dafny-lang/dafny/blob/master/Source/DafnyStandardLibraries/src/Std/Collections/Seq.dfy#L1026" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Sort Example</a></p>
<p><a href="https://gist.github.com/hath995/008921599add4c3a1a980c90da9af3c5" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Module Example</a></p>
<p>Note: I think the ... syntax was deprecated moving from Dafny 3 to 4.</p>

Q: com.amazonaws.SdkClientException: Unable to execute HTTP request: Connect to dynamodb.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com:443 A: <h3>With proxy</h3>
<p>If you want to setup a connection to DynamoDB through a proxy, be careful how you configure your pricy settings:</p>
<pre><code>ClientConfiguration clientConfiguration = new ClientConfiguration();

clientConfiguration.setProxyHost(&quot;yourProxyHost&quot;);
clientConfiguration.setProxyPort(8080);
clientConfiguration.setProxyUsername(&quot;proxyUsername&quot;);
clientConfiguration.setProxyPassword(&quot;proxyPassword&quot;);


AmazonDynamoDB client = AmazonDynamoDBClientBuilder.standard()
                .withCredentials(new AWSStaticCredentialsProvider(credentials))
                .withClientConfiguration(clientConfiguration)
                .withRegion(Regions.YOUR_REGION)
                .build();
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaSDK/latest/javadoc/com/amazonaws/ClientConfiguration.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaSDK/latest/javadoc/com/amazonaws/ClientConfiguration.html</a></p>
<h3>Without proxy</h3>
<p>If you are facing similar issue but not utilizing a proxy connection, then I suggest testing your host has a valid network path the the internet, or if in a private subnet on AWS it has a DynamoDB VPC Endpoint attached.</p>
